 "interpretationRules": {
  "encodedRules": [],
  "sourceGuidance": [
   {
    "title": "A-DES Scoring & Interpretation",
    "text": "The A-DES is scored by calculating the mean of all 30 items, resulting in a total score ranging from 0 to 10. Higher scores indicate greater frequency and severity of dissociative experiences.\n\nThe four A-DES subscale scores can be calculated to provide a more nuanced understanding of the adolescent’s dissociative experiences:\n1. Dissociative amnesia (items: 2, 5, 8, 12, 15, 22, 27): assesses gaps in memory and awareness.\n2. Absorption and imaginative involvement (items 1, 7, 10, 18, 24, 28): measures the tendency to become highly engrossed in activities with reduced awareness of surroundings.\n3. Passive influence (items 4, 14, 16, 19, 23): examines experiences of not having volitional control over one’s body and sensations.\n4. Depersonalisation and derealization (items 3, 6, 9, 11, 13, 17, 20, 21, 25, 26, 29, 30): reflects feelings of disconnection from one’s body and the world.\nNovoPsych have created the following guidelines to assist in interpretation:\nLow (0-1.9): Minimal dissociative experiences; typical of non-clinical adolescents.\nMild (2.0-2.9): Some dissociative experiences but generally within normal limits for adolescents.\nModerate (3.0-3.9): Notable dissociative experiences that may indicate elevated risk.\nHigh (4.0 and above): Significant dissociative experiences consistent with clinical levels.\nThe total score is often expressed as a percentile rank based on normative data from non-clinical adolescent samples. This percentile contextualises the adolescent’s score relative to peers, indicating the percentage of adolescents who scored lower. For example, a score at the 85th percentile means that 85% of adolescents in the normative sample scored lower, placing the individual in the upper range of dissociative experiences.\n\nOn first administration a stacked bar graph showing total and subscale average scores is displayed, along with a comparison graph illustrating the client’s scores relative to different reference groups.\n\nWhen the assessment is administered multiple times, a longitudinal line graph is generated to track changes in the total score.\n\nIn addition to a subscale average score graph over time.\n\nWhen tracking clinical progress, a meaningful change is defined as an increase or decrease of at least half a standard deviation in the total score (approximately .9 points on the 0-10 scale) This criterion is based on the Minimally Important Difference (MID) calculation (Turner et al., 2010). Such changes indicate meaningful improvement or reduction in symptoms, while a change of less than the specified points suggests no meaningful change in symptom severity between assessments. Regular monitoring of A-DES scores can help evaluate treatment effectiveness and guide intervention.\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
